                <pb facs="0483" n="387" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg387"/>

                <head>No. 288.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Nelson to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-02-06"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 6, 1872</date>.
                        (Received February 23.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 512.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: On the 29th ultimo General Neri,
                    commandingthe federal forces in the State of Zacatecas, suffered a defeat near
                    Fresnillo, in consequence of which he was obliged to evacuate the City of
                    Zacatecas and retire with the state government to Aguas Calientes. The city was
                    immediately occupied by the insurgents under General Guerra.</p>
                <p>News has been received of the death of General Felix Diaz, late governor of
                    Oaxaca, and brother of Portfirio Diaz. He had fled to the southward on the
                    taking of Oaxaca, expecting to embark in a vessel which he had at Puerto Angel,
                    but not succeeding in this, he sought refuge with a few attendants in the
                    mountains, where he was hunted down by forces from Tehauntepec, who were
                    bitterly hostile to him. He was captured alive, but was shot before being
                    presented at headquarters.</p>
                <p>* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>Robberies continue to abound in the vicinity of this city. Two days since the
                    Vera Cruz Railway was again robbed at Paso Ancho.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>THOMAS H. NELSON.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
